@page "/form-combo"

<CatHead Title="Combo fields">
    <code>.form-combo</code> is a field you type into to pick something: a searchable select, a
    multiselect whose picks are chips, a list the app searches on the server, and free entry that turns
    what is typed into chips. It is markup and classes like everything else &mdash;
    <code>data-combo</code> adds the keyboard.
</CatHead>

<div class="callout callout--info">
    <i class="ri-information-line"></i>
    <div class="callout-body">
        <span class="callout-title">The page owns the selection</span>
        In a Blazor app the chips and the options render from the app's state, and the script turns key
        presses into the clicks and changes that markup already handles: <kbd class="kbd">Enter</kbd>
        clicks the option, a second <kbd class="kbd">Backspace</kbd> clicks the last chip's dismiss
        button. The live examples below carry <code>data-combo-managed</code>, which lets the script keep
        the selection itself for a page with no app behind it. <strong>Leave it off wherever C# renders
        the chips</strong>, or both add one.
    </div>
</div>

<CatExample TExample="Examples.FormCombo.Multi" Title="Multiselect with chips" Demo="ex-demo--block ex-demo--pad">
    <p>
        <strong>Live.</strong> Each pick is a <code>.chip</code> inside <code>.form-combo-box</code>, and the
        list stays open while you pick. A chip goes by its &times;, by unticking its row, or by
        <kbd class="kbd">Backspace</kbd> twice in the empty input &mdash; the first press only marks it, so
        holding the key to fix a typo never eats the chips in front of it.
    </p>
    <p>
        <code>aria-multiselectable="true"</code> on the list is what draws the checkboxes. Open is
        <code>aria-expanded="true"</code> on the input, and nothing else: the attribute a screen reader
        announces is the one that shows the panel.
    </p>
</CatExample>

<CatExample TExample="Examples.FormCombo.Single" Title="Searchable select" Demo="ex-demo--block ex-demo--pad">
    <p>
        <strong>Live.</strong> <code>.form-combo-value</code> holds the chosen label, under the input in the
        same cell, so it reads like a <code>.form-select</code> until you type. The input needs a
        <code>placeholder</code> &mdash; <code>:placeholder-shown</code> is how the stylesheet knows nothing
        is typed. A managed field writes the value into its <code>input[data-combo-field]</code>; a Blazor
        page renders <code>.form-combo-value</code> from its own state instead.
    </p>
</CatExample>

<CatExample TExample="Examples.FormCombo.SingleWithIcon" Title="A searchable select with a leading icon" Demo="ex-demo--block ex-demo--pad">
    <p>
        A <code>.form-combo-icon</code> before the value takes a column of its own, so the value, the
        typed query and the actions keep their places beside it.
    </p>
</CatExample>

<CatExample TExample="Examples.FormCombo.TreeSingle" Title="Picking from a tree" Demo="ex-demo--block ex-demo--pad">
    <p>
        <strong>Live.</strong> A <a href="/tree"><code>.tree</code></a> in the panel in place of the
        listbox, and <code>aria-haspopup="tree"</code> on the input. The keyboard stays in the input:
        <kbd class="kbd">&darr;</kbd> <kbd class="kbd">&uarr;</kbd> walk the visible items,
        <kbd class="kbd">&rarr;</kbd> and <kbd class="kbd">&larr;</kbd> open and close the branch while
        nothing is typed, and <kbd class="kbd">Enter</kbd> picks. Typing hides every branch with no match
        and opens the ones holding one. Each item needs an <code>id</code>, for
        <code>aria-activedescendant</code>, and <code>aria-selected</code> if it can be picked &mdash; a
        hub here can be picked as well as an aisle.
    </p>
</CatExample>

<CatExample TExample="Examples.FormCombo.TreeChecks" Title="Ticking whole branches" Demo="ex-demo--block ex-demo--pad">
    <p>
        <strong>Live.</strong> <code>data-combo="multi"</code> over a tree that ticks: ticking a hub ticks
        its docks, and a managed field shows a fully ticked branch as one chip rather than one per dock.
        Dismissing a chip unticks what it stands for. <code>data-label</code> on an item is the chip's
        text when the row's own label needs its parent to make sense.
    </p>
</CatExample>

<CatExample TExample="Examples.FormCombo.GroupAction" Title="Selecting a whole group" Demo="ex-demo--block ex-demo--pad">
    <p>
        <strong>Live.</strong> A <code>.form-combo-group</code> heading holds one
        <code>.form-combo-group-action</code>: the group's name on the left, a small button on the right,
        set in the panel's own case rather than the heading's capitals. With
        <code>data-combo-toggle-group</code> the script clicks every enabled row of the group that is not
        ticked yet &mdash; or, once all of them are, every ticked one &mdash; so an option's own click handler
        is the whole integration, and a search that hides a row does not keep it out of the group.
    </p>
    <p>
        The script marks a heading <code>data-all-selected</code> while every enabled row under it is
        ticked, which shows <code>.form-combo-group-unpick</code> in place of
        <code>.form-combo-group-pick</code>. Focus stays in the input, as it does for an option: the arrow
        keys stop on the action between the rows and <kbd class="kbd">Enter</kbd> presses it, so give it
        an <code>id</code> and <code>tabindex="-1"</code>. Name it with <code>aria-labelledby</code> pointing
        at itself and the group's name, so a reader hears which group it selects. Without
        <code>data-combo-toggle-group</code> the button is the app's own, for any other one action.
    </p>
</CatExample>

<CatExample TExample="Examples.FormCombo.PanelWidth" Title="A panel wider than its field" Demo="ex-demo--block ex-demo--pad">
    <p>
        <strong>Live.</strong> In a narrow form column a long option is cut, and two names that differ only
        at the end cannot be told apart. <code>--form-combo-panel-width</code> on the field opens the list
        wider than the box; <code>.form-combo--fit</code> sizes it to its longest row, up to 36rem. Either
        way the panel is never narrower than the box, never wider than the viewport, and opens back
        towards the start when the end side has no room for it.
    </p>
    <p>
        <code>.form-combo--wrap</code> lets an option's <code>.form-combo-option-text</code> run onto a
        second line instead of ending in an ellipsis &mdash; for a phone, where even the widest panel is
        too narrow.
    </p>
    <p>
        <strong>On a phone, a sheet.</strong> Below 768px the panel opens as a sheet on the bottom
        edge of the screen, its full width and clear of the home indicator, whatever the width knobs
        say; the field keeps the focus, and a tap outside closes the list. A
        <code>.form-combo--inline</code> list stays in the flow. On a page whose viewport does not
        resize for the on-screen keyboard, the keyboard covers that edge &mdash; set
        <code>interactive-widget=resizes-content</code> in the viewport meta.
    </p>
</CatExample>

<CatExample TExample="Examples.FormCombo.OpensUpward" Title="A list that opens upward" Demo="ex-demo--block ex-demo--pad">
    <p>
        <strong>Live &mdash; type in it.</strong> A field in a bar at the foot of a pane opens its
        list above itself with <code>--panel-placement: block-start</code> on the bar, the
        <code>.form-combo</code> or anything above them &mdash; the token every anchored panel reads,
        with the <a href="/menu#where-it-opens">menu's</a> four values. Where there is no room above
        it still opens below, and on a phone it is a sheet as before.
    </p>
</CatExample>

<CatExample TExample="Examples.FormCombo.FreeEntry" Title="Free entry" Demo="ex-demo--block ex-demo--pad">
    <p>
        <strong>Live &mdash; try pasting <code>ops@example.org, billing@example.net; ops@example</code>.</strong>
        <code>data-combo="free"</code> has no list. <kbd class="kbd">Enter</kbd>, a comma, a semicolon,
        leaving the field and pasting all commit; a pasted list is split and entries already there are
        skipped. An address field also splits on spaces, which an address never holds.
    </p>
    <p>
        <code>.chip--invalid</code> keeps a rejected entry as a chip rather than throwing the typo away;
        clicking its label in a managed field puts it back in the input. A managed field checks addresses
        with <code>data-combo-validate="email"</code>, which, unlike <code>type="email"</code>, rejects a
        domain with no dot.
    </p>
</CatExample>

<CatExample TExample="Examples.FormCombo.Collapse" Title="Collapsed until focused" Demo="ex-demo--block ex-demo--pad">
    <p>
        <code>.form-combo--collapse</code> keeps a long set of picks to one row while the field is not in
        use: the first three chips, then a count of the rest from a CSS counter. Focus it and every chip
        wraps back and the list opens, because the reader came to change the picks. Without the modifier the box grows a row at a time, which is right in a form where the
        picks are the thing being reviewed.
    </p>
</CatExample>

<CatExample TExample="Examples.FormCombo.ServerStates" Title="Server-backed states" Demo="ex-demo--block ex-demo--pad">
    <p>
        <code>data-combo-source="server"</code> tells the script the app filters, so it hides no rows. The
        states are the app's markup: <code>.form-combo-skeleton</code> rows for the first page,
        <code>aria-busy="true"</code> on the list while a query runs &mdash; which dims the rows that are
        there and shows <code>.form-combo-busy</code> &mdash;, <code>.form-combo-empty</code> when a result
        came back empty, and <code>.form-combo-status--error</code> with a retry.
    </p>
    <p>
        <code>.form-combo-more</code>, straight after the list, asks for the next page &mdash; after it
        rather than in it, because a listbox may hold only options. It is a real button, and the script
        clicks it once the list is scrolled to its end. These are drawn with
        <code>.form-combo--inline</code>, which keeps the list open in the flow &mdash; the shape for a
        picker that is the whole content of a drawer or a popover.
    </p>
</CatExample>

<CatSnippet Name="FormCombo/MultiInBlazor.txt" Title="A multiselect in Blazor">
    <p>
        The whole list is on the page, so the script filters it and the component binds nothing to the
        input. <code>@@onclick</code> on an option and on a chip's dismiss button is the integration;
        <code>aria-selected</code> is rendered as the string <code>"true"</code> or <code>"false"</code>,
        because a Razor boolean attribute is written with no value at all.
    </p>
</CatSnippet>

<CatSnippet Name="FormCombo/GroupActionInBlazor.txt" Title="Selecting a whole group in Blazor">
    <p>
        Nothing is bound to the group's button. The script clicks the rows that have to change, and each
        click reaches <code>Toggle</code> as a pointer's would &mdash; one event per row, in the order they are
        listed. A page that would rather take the whole group in one call leaves
        <code>data-combo-toggle-group</code> off and puts its own <code>@@onclick</code> on the button.
    </p>
</CatSnippet>

<CatSnippet Name="FormCombo/ServerInBlazor.txt" Title="A server-backed list in Blazor">
    <p>
        The picks render from the record being edited, not from the search results, so a tag already on
        the note is a chip before any search has run. The query is bound on <code>oninput</code> and
        debounced in C#, and a superseded search is cancelled rather than allowed to overwrite a newer one.
    </p>
</CatSnippet>

<CatSnippet Name="FormCombo/FreeEntryInBlazor.txt" Title="Free entry in Blazor">
    <p>
        One <code>change</code> arrives per entry, already split and de-duplicated, and the script clears
        the input afterwards. The platform's own <code>change</code> on leaving the field is replaced by
        the same commit, so a typed entry is never added twice.
    </p>
</CatSnippet>

<h2 id="keyboard">Keyboard <CatAnchor Id="keyboard" /></h2>
<div class="sedna-scroll-x">
    <table class="table">
        <thead>
            <tr><th>Key</th><th>Single</th><th>Multi</th><th>Free entry</th></tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
            <tr><td><kbd class="kbd">↓</kbd> <kbd class="kbd">↑</kbd></td><td>open, then move</td><td>open, then move, stopping on a group's action</td><td>move through suggestions, if any</td></tr>
            <tr><td><kbd class="kbd">Enter</kbd></td><td>pick and close</td><td>tick or untick, stay open; on a group's action, press it</td><td>add what was typed</td></tr>
            <tr><td><kbd class="kbd">,</kbd> <kbd class="kbd">;</kbd> paste</td><td>&mdash;</td><td>&mdash;</td><td>add; a pasted list is split</td></tr>
            <tr><td><kbd class="kbd">Backspace</kbd> in an empty input</td><td>&mdash;</td><td>mark the last chip, then remove it</td><td>mark the last chip, then remove it</td></tr>
            <tr><td><kbd class="kbd">Esc</kbd></td><td>close and clear the query</td><td>close and clear the query</td><td>clear what was typed</td></tr>
            <tr><td><kbd class="kbd">Tab</kbd></td><td>close</td><td>close</td><td>add what was typed, then move on</td></tr>
        </tbody>
    </table>
</div>
